# Force LF line endings for all text files, regardless of each developer's
# local core.autocrlf setting. Added 2026-06-29 — root cause of a real test
# failure: tests/snapshot.test.ts compares buildSystemPrompt()/buildUserPrompt()
# output (always LF, generated in-memory) against tests/snapshots/*.txt read
# via bare readFileSync with no CRLF normalization. On a Windows checkout with
# core.autocrlf=true, Git silently rewrites the committed LF snapshot files to
# CRLF on checkout — the test then fails on every blank line (a lone \r renders
# in terminal diff output as a trailing space, since \r is a cursor-return
# control character, not a literal character — this masked non-blank-line
# mismatches by visually overwriting them, leaving only blank lines as visible
# diffs). The repository's committed files were always correct (confirmed: a
# fresh Linux clone has clean LF, zero CRLF, all tests pass) — this was a
# local-checkout artifact, not a content bug. `text=auto` lets Git auto-detect
# binary files and leave them untouched; `eol=lf` pins the checkout encoding
# for genuine text files so this can't recur on any platform.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Explicit for the files that actually broke, so the rule's intent is clear
# even if the blanket rule above is ever narrowed:
tests/snapshots/*.txt text eol=lf
